    "constrained by many of the same factors that limit male-led MSMEs, such as deficient infrastructure, including power, transportation, water and telecommunications and a gap in access to and use of finance (Stein, Hommes and Pinar Ardic, 2013; Beck and Cull, 2014; Crick, Gannon, et al., 2018). Yet participants in our research identified Page 10 of 18 several factors that they suggested constituted additional barriers to adaptation for female-led MSMEs in Kenya’s SALs. In general terms, respondent",
    "additional barriers to adaptation for female-led MSMEs in Kenya’s SALs. In general terms, respondents emphasised that socially ‘institutionalized’ gender roles are a major challenge to female entrepreneurship, with the burden of caring for children and running internal family affairs, as well as meeting household livelihood needs, most often falling on women in Narok. Participants emphasized that these responsibilities present a high opportunity cost, in terms of reducing the time available for",
    "these responsibilities present a high opportunity cost, in terms of reducing the time available for women to invest in business development; including in identifying and executing strategies to build resilience to climate risk within their MSMEs. They also emphasized that the additional emotional pressures that arise from facing these responsibilities under conditions of uncertain food and financial security form an additional barrier to adaptation and cited physical impacts on their health, suc",
