    "BUSINESS MANAGEMENT SKILLS GUIDE FOR KENYAN MSMEs Sources: FKE, KIPPRA, Strathmore Business School, World Bank === WHY MANAGEMENT SKILLS MATTER === - 3 out of 5 Kenyan businesses fail within the first few months (KNBS) - 400,000 SMEs fail within 2 years of inception (KNBS 2021) - Main reasons: Poor management, inadequate record-keeping, lack of planning === 1. BUSINESS PLANNING === What is a Business Plan? A written document that outlines your business goals and how to achieve them. Key Sections",
    "ess Plan? A written document that outlines your business goals and how to achieve them. Key Sections of a Kenya Business Plan: 1. Executive Summary — What your business does, where it is located 2. Business Description — Products/services, legal structure, location 3. Market Analysis — Who are your customers? Who are your competitors? 4. Marketing Strategy — How will you attract and keep customers? 5. Operations Plan — How will you run the business day to day? 6. Financial Plan — Revenue forecas",
    "? 5. Operations Plan — How will you run the business day to day? 6. Financial Plan — Revenue forecast, expenses, profit projections 7. Funding Requirements — How much money do you need and for what? Why You Need a Business Plan: - Required by banks and SACCOs when applying for loans - Helps you think clearly about your business - Guides your decisions when problems arise - Helps you set and measure goals FREE HELP: Kenya Small Business Development Centers (SBDC) at Strathmore offer free business",
    "e goals FREE HELP: Kenya Small Business Development Centers (SBDC) at Strathmore offer free business plan development assistance for MSMEs. === 2. FINANCIAL RECORD KEEPING === Basic Records Every MSME Must Keep: 1. Sales record — all money received 2. Purchase record — all money spent 3. Cash book — daily cash in and out 4. Stock record — inventory levels 5. Customer credit book — money owed to you 6. Supplier credit book — money you owe others Why Keep Records? - KRA can audit your business up",
    "u 6. Supplier credit book — money you owe others Why Keep Records? - KRA can audit your business up to 7 years back - Banks require financial records for loan applications - Helps you know if your business is profitable - Required for TOT and VAT filing Simple Tools: - Exercise book and pen (minimum) - Microsoft Excel spreadsheet - Mobile apps: M-Ledger, Wave Accounting (free) - Accounting software: QuickBooks, Sage (paid) === 3. FINANCIAL MANAGEMENT === Cash Flow Management: - Know your break-e",
    "e: QuickBooks, Sage (paid) === 3. FINANCIAL MANAGEMENT === Cash Flow Management: - Know your break-even point (minimum sales to cover costs) - Keep 3 months of expenses as emergency reserve - Separate personal and business finances - Pay yourself a salary — don't just take from the till Pricing Your Products: - Cost + Mark-up = Selling Price - Mark-up should cover: materials, labour, overhead, profit - Research what competitors charge - Consider your target customer's ability to pay Managing Deb",
    "fit - Research what competitors charge - Consider your target customer's ability to pay Managing Debt: - Avoid taking loans for consumption (food, rent) - Borrow only for income-generating investment - Keep loan payments below 30% of monthly income - Pay Hustler Fund and SACCO loans on time to build credit score === 4. MARKETING FOR KENYAN MSMEs === Low-Cost Marketing Strategies: 1. Word of mouth — happy customers tell others 2. WhatsApp Business — free, reaches customers directly 3. Facebook/In",
    "— happy customers tell others 2. WhatsApp Business — free, reaches customers directly 3. Facebook/Instagram — free business pages 4. Business cards — cheap and professional 5. Signage — visible shop sign attracts walk-in customers 6. Market days — participate in local market events 7. Referral incentives — reward customers who bring others Digital Marketing (Ajira Digital training available): - Social media marketing - Google My Business listing (free) - E-commerce on Jumia, Kilimall, Facebook M",
    "cial media marketing - Google My Business listing (free) - E-commerce on Jumia, Kilimall, Facebook Marketplace - WhatsApp catalogue for product display === 5. HUMAN RESOURCE MANAGEMENT === Hiring Staff: - Start with family and trusted community members - Apprenticeship model — train young people on the job - NITA apprenticeship scheme — get government support - Interns from TVET institutions — lower cost, fresh skills Managing Workers: - Set clear duties and expectations - Pay on time — trust is",
    "er cost, fresh skills Managing Workers: - Set clear duties and expectations - Pay on time — trust is key - Register workers with NSSF and NHIF (now SHA) — legal requirement - Deduct PAYE from salaries and remit to KRA === 6. TECHNOLOGY ADOPTION FOR MSMEs === Must-Have Technologies: 1. M-Pesa Paybill or Till — for receiving payments 2. WhatsApp Business — for customer communication 3. Mobile banking — for managing business finances 4. eTIMS — for issuing KRA-compliant invoices 5. Safaricom Bonga",
    "g — for managing business finances 4. eTIMS — for issuing KRA-compliant invoices 5. Safaricom Bonga Points Business — loyalty rewards Intermediate Technologies: - Social media presence (Facebook, Instagram, TikTok) - E-commerce listing on Jumia or Kilimall - Online accounting software Advanced Technologies: - Website for your business - Point of Sale (POS) system - ERP software for larger businesses === 7. SKILLS GAPS IN KENYAN MSMEs (FKE Research) === Most Common Skill Gaps Identified: 1. Digit",
    "es === 7. SKILLS GAPS IN KENYAN MSMEs (FKE Research) === Most Common Skill Gaps Identified: 1. Digital literacy — use of computers and internet 2. Financial management — bookkeeping, budgeting 3. Marketing and customer service 4. Technical/vocational skills in specific sectors 5. English language and communication 6. Leadership and management Skill development resources: - NITA — technical and vocational training - TVETA accredited institutions — certificate courses - Ajira Digital — free digita",
    "ational training - TVETA accredited institutions — certificate courses - Ajira Digital — free digital skills training - Strathmore SBDC — free business management training - KBA (Kenya Bankers Association) — financial literacy === 8. GOVERNMENT SUPPORT FOR MANAGEMENT TRAINING === Available Programs: - TVET Authority — accredited training institutions nationwide - NITA — industrial training with levy reimbursement - Ajira Digital — 391,000 youth trained by June 2024 - MSEA — micro and small enter",
    "vy reimbursement - Ajira Digital — 391,000 youth trained by June 2024 - MSEA — micro and small enterprise support programs - County Governments — some offer free business training Key Contact: - Ministry of Labour: labour.go.ke - MSEA: msea.go.ke - NITA: nita.go.ke",
